---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Stamp from '../components/Stamp.astro';
import { data, verifiedCount, pairExtra, formatDateLong, countryByCode, pairPath } from '../lib/data';
const verifiedFmt = verifiedCount.toLocaleString('en-US');
const jp = countryByCode['JP'];
const th = countryByCode['TH'];
const zw = countryByCode['ZW'];
const ve = countryByCode['VE'];
const exVerified = pairExtra('JP', 'TH');
const exPending = pairExtra('ZW', 'VE');
---

<Base
  title="About the Data — How visafreely Verifies Visa Requirements"
  description="Where visafreely's visa data comes from, how pairs are verified against official sources, and what the 'Last verified' stamp does and does not promise."
>
  <h1>About the data</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Visa information is only useful if you can tell how fresh it is. This page explains exactly where
    our data comes from and how it is checked.
  </p>

  <h2>The two stamps</h2>
  <p>
    Every pair page on this site carries one of two stamps, directly under the answer. There is no
    third state, and no answer hides which one it has.
  </p>

  <p class="micro" style="margin-bottom:6px;">Stamp 1 — verified</p>
  <div class="seal-block verified" data-reveal>
    <div class="seal-art" aria-hidden="true">
      <Stamp variant="seal" size={64} />
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="seal-title">
        Verified — last checked {exVerified.verified && formatDateLong(exVerified.verified)} against an official source:{' '}
        <a href={exVerified.source} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{exVerified.source_name}</a>.
      </p>
      {exVerified.note && <p>{exVerified.note}</p>}
      {exVerified.changed && (
        <p>
          Value last changed {formatDateLong(exVerified.changed)} — see the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>.
        </p>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    Live example: <a href={pairPath(jp, th)}>{jp.name} passport → {th.name}</a>. The date is the day the
    check was completed; the source is named and linked, so you can re-check it yourself.
  </p>

  <p class="micro" style="margin:18px 0 6px;">Stamp 2 — dataset, not yet verified</p>
  <div class="seal-block pending" data-reveal>
    <div class="seal-art" aria-hidden="true">
      <Stamp variant="seal" state="pending" size={64} />
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="seal-title">Dataset — not yet individually verified</p>
      <p>
        Data from the passport-index dataset, {data.vintage} snapshot
        {exPending.overridden ? ' — with a correction applied by visafreely (see the changelog)' : ''}.
        This pair has not yet been individually verified against an official source; see
        <a href="/about/"> how verification works</a>.
      </p>
      {exPending.note && <p>Note: {exPending.note}</p>}
      {exPending.changed && <p>This value was last changed on {formatDateLong(exPending.changed)}.</p>}
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    Live example: <a href={pairPath(zw, ve)}>{zw.name} passport → {ve.name}</a>. The dashed stamp is not
    an apology — it is the pair's place in the verification queue, stated on the page instead of hidden.
  </p>

  <h2>How verification works</h2>
  <div class="how-grid cols-2">
    <div class="how-card">
      <span class="num">1</span>
      <h3>Find the official source</h3>
      <p>The foreign ministry, immigration authority or official embassy page for the route — never another visa site.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="how-card">
      <span class="num">2</span>
      <h3>Check the pair</h3>
      <p>Compare the dataset value against that source: the requirement itself and the permitted stay.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="how-card">
      <span class="num">3</span>
      <h3>Stamp it</h3>
      <p>Record the check date and the source on the pair page. If the source disagrees with the dataset, the value is corrected first.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="how-card">
      <span class="num">4</span>
      <h3>Publish corrections</h3>
      <p>Every change lands in the <a href="/changelog/">data changelog</a> — old value, new value, date and official source.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The base dataset</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0;">
      The starting point is the open <strong>passport-index dataset</strong> (MIT licence), which maps the
      visa requirement for every passport → destination pair: visa free (with the permitted stay),
      visa on arrival, e-Visa, electronic travel authorisation, visa required, or no admission. We use the
      February 2026 snapshot as the base layer for all {data.pairCount.toLocaleString('en-US')} pairs.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0;">
      A snapshot is a photograph, not a feed — visa rules change every month, and the snapshot is already
      wrong in places the day it is taken. So the snapshot is never the final word on this site.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The verification layer</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0;">
      We check pairs individually against primary official sources — foreign ministries, immigration
      authorities and official embassy pages — starting with the most-travelled routes, and record the
      date and the source for every check. A pair page shows a <strong>“Last verified”</strong> date only
      when such a check has actually been completed for that pair. Pairs not yet checked say so plainly
      and show the dataset's vintage instead. At launch, {verifiedFmt} pairs carry a
      verified stamp; the rest are queued in a published order of travel importance.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0;">
      When a check finds the dataset is wrong, we correct it and publish the change — old value, new
      value, date and official source — in the <a href="/changelog/">data changelog</a>. Some rules carry
      an announced end date (temporary visa-free policies are common); where an official source gives one,
      the pair page shows it as a scheduled change.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the stamp does not promise</h2>
  <p>
    “Last verified” means the requirement matched the named official source on that date. It does not
    guarantee entry: border officers decide admission, rules can change between the check and your trip,
    and pages here cover ordinary passports on short tourist visits — not work, study, residence or
    special passport categories. Always confirm with the destination's embassy or official immigration
    authority before travelling, especially if your trip depends on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Found something that disagrees with an official source? That is exactly the feedback the changelog
    runs on — every confirmed discrepancy becomes a correction entry with its source attached.
  </p>
</Base>
